# uml quality entry Tool Spec (#93)

Purpose: WSL Ubuntu invocation surface for the adjacent uml-viewer quality dispatch (ADR 0068 / ADR 0073). CRAP and mutation run through uml-viewer's standard tools (crapper / mutator Lua paths); this repository owns only the scope config, the pinned Lua environment and the preflight.

Upstream (adjacent checkout, ignored by git): `.uml-viewer/uml-viewer/scripts/uml_posix.py`, `.uml-viewer/crapper`, `.uml-viewer/mutator`, installed by uml-viewer's `get-uml-viewer`. `UML_VIEWER_TOOL_ROOT` selects a development checkout instead of the installed copy. These repositories are read-only from this project.
Project wrapper: `lua5.4 tools/cli.lua uml <check|crap|mutate|ir|view> [args]` (package `tools/packages/uml_quality/`).

CLI contract:
- `check` runs the preflight only: exit 0 when everything resolves, 1 otherwise.
- `crap` / `mutate` run the preflight, then `python3 -I -B <tool-root>/scripts/uml_posix.py command <root> crap|mutate [args]` inside a pinned sh launch script. `crap` passes its exit code through unchanged (0/2 are upstream valid results).
- `.uml-viewer/quality-result.json` is echoed as upstream diagnostics. For `mutate`, its `validResult` is never the passing criterion. The wrapper resolves the same normalized targets through `mutation_targets.py` (upstream `uml_quality.targets`), records each artifact's file identity (mtime/ctime/inode/content digest) before dispatch without deleting incremental history, then independently checks every selected target after dispatch:
  - Effective result: a newly published `.metrics/mutate/<module>.edn` with matching source/namespace, nonnegative consistent form statistics and positive killed + survived count. Exit 0; print target, mutation sites, killed, survived and uncovered counts. Upstream 3 (survivors) is normalized to 0 only after this check passes.
  - No sites: a successful read-only `mutator --scan --no-coverage <same normalized target>` reports exactly zero sites and the Lua syntax tree is valid. Exit 0, print `status=no-sites` and target. A missing snapshot may take this branch; an unchanged existing snapshot remains a stale artifact and fails.
  - Not executed: missing/stale/malformed snapshot, wrong target, missing/inconsistent statistics, only uncovered sites, failed/mismatched scan, syntax errors, or upstream exit other than 0/3. Exit 1 and name the target. A nonzero scan with missing `.edn` is never no-sites.
- Lua module mapping follows crapper: remove the first leading `src/lua/`, `src/` or `lua/`, remove `.lua` and terminal `/init`, replace `/` with `.` (preserve `_`). The checker uses mutator's EDN syntax reader, not its valid-result logic. CLI/process tests cover missing, stale, valid, no-sites and failure evidence. Changes to the UML quality wrapper require the real Python process suite in a fully installed environment: first run `lua5.4 tools/cli.lua uml check`; only after it succeeds, run `.uml-viewer/mutator/.venv/bin/python -I -B -m unittest discover -s tools/packages/uml_quality/test -p test_mutation_evidence.py`. Default tooling runs the Lua CLI tests; the Python suite is an explicit validation step. A missing environment means the Python suite was not executed, never passed or skipped.
- Preflight failure exits 1 (never 3), prints `FAIL` lines, deletes any stale `.uml-viewer/quality-result.json` and starts no tool.
- Missing or unknown operation exits 2.
- The installer copies `uml` into the repo root. They are ignored and are not the supported entry, because they neither pin the Lua search path nor run the controlled-environment preflight. The one exception is inside the companion started by `uml view` (below), which inherits the pinned environment.

`ir` contract (#99 / ADR 0070):
- Takes no extra arguments (exit 2 otherwise).
- Projects `arch_policy.project_uml_policy` from `tools/packages/arch_policy/policy.json`, then merges `:proposals` and `:omit` from the versioned override `docs/architecture.proposals.edn` (`packages.uml_quality.proposals`), and sets `:out "tmp/uml/monopoly.edn"`. The merge never writes back to `docs/architecture.md` or the guards.
- Override shape: one top-level map with only `:proposals` and `:omit`, each a vector, copied verbatim. A missing, empty or comment-only file means `[]` for both. Malformed EDN, any other key, a duplicate key or a non-vector value exits 1 with a `docs/architecture.proposals.edn (line N): ...` message. In that case `policy.edn` is not written and nothing is started.
- Preflight: Python 3.11+, the uml-viewer checkout, and no repo-root `examples/`. Upstream `ir` prefers `examples/*.policy.edn` over `policy.edn`, so an `examples/` directory could shadow the generated file. A preflight failure exits 1 and writes nothing.
- Then it writes the repo-root `policy.edn` (ignored by git) and runs the POSIX entry with `ir policy.edn tmp/uml/monopoly.edn`. Upstream `ir` takes `[policy [output]]` positionally, so both are passed explicitly; a lone argument would be read as the policy and the output would fall back to `examples/uml-viewer.edn`. The exit code is passed through unchanged.

`view` contract (#99 / ADR 0070):
- Preflight: the same Lua checks as `check` (native `lua5.4`, controlled Lua environment, module probe), Python 3.11+, the uml-viewer checkout with `uml-quality.json`, and Herdr. Upstream selects the highest `herdr` on `PATH` whose `herdr --version` is `herdr X.Y.Z` >= 0.9.3 with protocol 22. The preflight applies the same version rule to every `PATH` candidate and requires at least one. The protocol check stays upstream's. Nothing is downloaded or upgraded.
- A preflight failure exits 1 with `FAIL` lines and does not start the upstream launcher. It leaves `.uml-viewer/quality-result.json` alone.
- Then a pinned sh script runs the POSIX entry with `<root> [args]` (args forwarded, e.g. `--restart`). The script carries the same pinned Lua environment as `crap` / `mutate` (`runtime.pinned_env`). The inherited `PATH` keeps its order, so upstream's Herdr selection sees the user's installs unchanged. The variables are set only inside the child sh script, so the parent process, the global Herdr and Lua setup and the read-only upstream checkout are unchanged. Upstream creates a fresh Herdr server per launch, and the environment flows down to the companion and its shells. The exit code is passed through unchanged.
- Window, companion, CRAP colouring and Herdr cleanup are verified manually on WSL Ubuntu by the maintainer. They are not covered by the automated suite.

Dispatch precedence (owned by uml-viewer `scripts/uml_posix.py`, recorded here): `operations` > `standardTools` > Clojure alias. The alias route is taken only when `deps.edn` exists and no `standardTools` is declared; with neither, the standard tools run with installed defaults. `uml-quality.json` declares `standardTools` only:
- `sourceRoots: ["src"]`: the whole `src/` tree is business code, the same scope as the retired crap4lua lane.
- `exclude: []` must stay declared. uml-viewer's mutation scope adapter wraps an absent `exclude` as `@($null)` and fails, so the empty array is part of the contract.
- No routing flags (`--root`, `--source-root`, `--exclude`, `--full-tree`, `--changed`, `--mutate-all`) in `crapperOptions` / `mutatorOptions`. uml-viewer rejects them.
- crap always runs `--full-tree`. The preflight validates the file with uml-viewer's own `read_config`, `scope` and `targets` modules through the POSIX port `probe_config.py` (`python3 -I -B`, tool root passed explicitly), not with a local re-implementation.

Pinned Lua environment (the false-green guard):
- A user tree outside the resolved set also carries busted / luacov / luacov-reporter-lcov, and the user `LUA_PATH` may point at it. A child that inherits it would "work" even when the packages come from a tree outside this repo's version rules.
- The controlled Lua environment is resolved once by `foundation.lua_env` through `luarocks config rocks_trees`; versions must satisfy busted `>=2.3.0,<3`, luassert `>=1.9.0,<2`, luacov `>=0.17.0,<1`, luacov-reporter-lcov `>=0.2,<1`, luacheck `>=1.2.0,<2`. The repo only verifies; it never installs or writes a tree.
- The child launch script sets `LUA_PATH(_5_4)` = repo root + every controlled tree's `share/lua/5.4` (`?.lua` and `?/init.lua`), `LUA_CPATH(_5_4)` = every controlled tree's `lib/lua/5.4` (`?.so`), empty `LUA_INIT(_5_4)`, and puts the `lua5.4` directory first on `PATH` (crapper resolves the interpreter from `PATH`).
- There is no `;;` default path. `luarocks.loader` must stay unresolvable, otherwise it would re-attach trees outside the resolved set.
- The preflight runs `probe_lua_modules.lua` under that environment. Every required module (`busted.runner`, `luassert`, `luacov.runner`, `luacov.reporter.lcov`) and every file loaded while requiring them, C modules included, must resolve inside one of the controlled trees.
- Missing packages or out-of-range versions are a real preflight failure (exit 1) with the installer command printed; there is no mirror, no copy from another tree and no write to any tree.

Python environment (crapper / mutator):
- Python 3.11+ (both pyprojects: `requires-python >=3.11`; deps `tree-sitter>=0.24`, `tree-sitter-language-pack>=0.7`).
- One venv per checkout, created by uml-viewer's documented commands:
  - `python3 -m venv .uml-viewer/crapper/.venv`, then `.uml-viewer/crapper/.venv/bin/python -m pip install -e .uml-viewer/crapper`
  - the same for `mutator` (plus `-e .uml-viewer/crapper`).
- The preflight runs each venv's `bin/python -I` and requires the version >= 3.11 and the package imported from its own checkout (`.uml-viewer/<tool>/src`). It also requires mutator's `ensure_crapper()` to resolve crapper from `.uml-viewer/crapper/src`.

Outputs and version control:
- crapper writes `.metrics/crap.edn` and `target/coverage/lua/lcov.info`; mutator writes `.metrics/mutate/*.edn`.
- Following the upstream README, `.metrics/` is versioned: metrics produced by real `lua5.4 tools/cli.lua uml crap|mutate` runs are committed. Never hand-write or fabricate metric files. `.metrics/` is a whitelisted root entry (`test/guards/lib/root_whitelist_guard.lua`).
- `ir` outputs stay out of version control. The repo-root `policy.edn` is listed in `.gitignore` and not whitelisted by the root guard. `tmp/uml/monopoly.edn` sits under the ignored `tmp/`. The override `docs/architecture.proposals.edn` is versioned and allowlisted by the docs guard.
- `target/` (coverage intermediates) stays ignored, like `.uml-viewer/` and `uml-viewer-log.txt` (installer-maintained block at the end of `.gitignore`).

Stock busted entry (`.busted`, #99):
- crapper / mutator take the Lua module root from the nearest `.busted` and run stock `busted.runner` there with `-c --coverage-config-file=<their luacov config>`, not `tools/packages/test_runner`. The repo-root `.busted` (whitelisted root entry) runs `test/behavior` + `test/contract` (`test_*` files, the specs covering `src/`), TAP output, and empties busted's default `./src` / `./csrc` lpath/cpath prefixes.
- Its helper `test/busted_helper.lua` adapts the stock runner to `test/helper.lua`'s lifecycle contract: `project/case/start` before the `before_each` chain (behavior RNG reseed), `project/case/end` after `after_each`, `project/suite/end`, and baseline leaks fail the run. In ordinary runs it adopts the runner's preloaded busted / luassert only when every search template lies under the repo root (or the mutation worker) plus the controlled Lua environment, and each module resolves inside one of the controlled trees (`cache_loader.adopt_preloaded`).
- Mutation workers use upstream's `MUTATOR_WORKER_ROOT` / `MUTATOR_SOURCE_ROOT` pair. Upstream sets both to absolute paths only for real workers and rebases repository Lua templates to the worker. The consumer requires cwd and the requested loader root to equal the worker, directly under `<source>/target/mutation-workers/run-*/worker-*`; the source must carry repository markers, and worker and controlled-tree directory components must be real directories, not links. Missing, partial or inconsistent context fails closed.
- Worker search templates may address worker source files and the controlled Lua environment only. Source repository code templates, other/foreign trees, external paths, path traversal and empty/default templates are rejected. After worker-local `test.bootstrap` adds paths, the helper installs the loader; subsequent `cache_loader.install(worker)` validates the same binding. Dependencies continue to load explicitly from the controlled trees and retain verified object identity. The worker never installs, copies or links a rocks tree through this entry. A missing worker source file cannot fall back to the source repository.
- Nested project runners use the same binding: `cache_loader.install(worker)` revalidates cwd/root, worker layout and search templates, then confines dependency loading to the controlled trees. Missing dependencies fail; worker runs never install or create a rocks tree. This covers the nested runners started by the default `log_warns_handler` behavior specs.
- Coverage names: crapper's luacov config drops absolute chunk names, and this repo puts the root on `package.path` as an absolute path. The helper therefore loads repo-local Lua files (outside the controlled trees) under relative forward-slash chunk names, and stores luacov stats under the native-separator names luacov's untested-file walk uses, so a source never appears as two LCOV records.
- The run does not pass through the in-repo parallel lanes: the whole behavior + contract set runs in one process (~100 s for `uml crap`).

Known gaps:
- Source mutation (`uml mutate`) scope excludes `src/ui/**` (ADR 0076); CRAP and dry still cover all of `src/`. A full run passes every other top-level `src/` directory explicitly: `lua5.4 tools/cli.lua uml mutate src/app src/computer src/config src/foundation src/host src/player src/rules src/state src/turn` (add new top-level directories here). Calling `uml mutate` without targets is rejected upstream and is not a full-run entry. Files in scope without a `.metrics/mutate` snapshot either have no mutation sites (data tables, port declarations) or have no mutation result yet.
